okay let's get to audience questions for  the week I've got some interesting ones  to discuss with you the first one is  about Donald Trump's fundraising for  2020 and I got this question in a few  different formats and the gist of it is  David how is it that Donald Trump is  raising such insanely huge amounts of  money for 2020 and there's a lot of  misinformation spreading about this  which I want to sort of break down for  you today number one a lot of the  reports of Trump's fundraising for 2020  are very overblown and there's a couple  different story lines that have been  circulating one is that Trump is raising  more money than all of the Democratic  candidates combined that's just flat-out  untrue Donald Trump right now has  roughly the same cash on hand as Bernie  and Kamala Harris two of the bigger  democratic fundraisers so far but it's  important to understand that aside from  it being incorrect that dropped Trump  has raised as much money as all of the  dem candidates which he hasn't a lot of  trumps fundraising has happened number  one because of these joint fundraising  agreements with the Republican Party  which the eventual Democratic nominee  will likely already have with the  Democratic Party but we're not seeing  the full force of that happening right  now because there are so many Democratic  candidates currently in the mix and  number two you have to understand that  Trump has been raising money for 2024  way longer than any Democratic candidate  remember that the day before the day of  the inauguration I believe it was of  Donald Trump's inauguration he  officially filed for reelection and I  told you then it's funny thinking back  to whatever the date was January 21 22  of 2017 this allowed Trump to  immediately start raising money for 2020  so Trump has a bunch of cash on hand  right now but he's been raising money  for more than two years at this point in  time because of that very early  re-election filing there's also just a  kind of simple mathematical thing that's  important to consider which is that if  you donate to Trump in January of 2017  or in March of 2017 or in May  of 2018 or whatever you're already  donating to the general election in the  sense that okay William weld is  challenging Trump for the Republican  nomination you may see a bit of a  Republican primary but in all likelihood  if you know so early that you can donate  to the candidate who will be on the  national election ballot that is going  to generate a lot more contributions  than primary donations where you might  end up donating to somebody and  mathematically most of the people  running on the Democratic side are not  going to be on the ballot on in November  of 2020 because they will have lost the  primary so that is just a mathematical  reality people often are much more  willing to donate to someone they know  will be on the ballot in the end then  one of 15 or however many Democratic  candidates there are who might not even  make it to the general election so those  are all sort of structural realities the  other thing that's really important to  mention and this is where we get sort of  into the qualitative stuff rather than  the quantitative with Trump's  fundraising is that Trump does a lot of  stupid things and he has a lot of  boneheaded people around him but he has  very cleverly used all of the scandals  which are correct scandals they're real  scandals they're all based on real stuff  that Trump did he's been doing a really  good job of framing them as fake and  then saying help me raise money in order  to fight back against the fake news the  witch-hunt Moeller probe which of course  confirmed in the Moller report almost  every major investigative story  surrounding the Moller probe trump for  two years now has been saying witch-hunt  illegal investigation partisan  investigation give me money to help me  fight back against it that is extracted  a lot of money from Trump's base  claiming to be the victim asking people  to donate on the basis of the Dems are  stopping me from building the wall when  in reality Republicans also were not  particularly thrilled with a lot of  Trump's wall related funding proposals  that is actually you've got a hand that  the Trump he's actually done that and  really it's the people around him  they've done that pretty effectively  none of this by the way changes that  it's ultimately going to be a turnout  election Trump won with 77,000 votes in  three states if the left can slightly  increase turnout in those key states  that could have gone one way or the  other it's over Trump has no chance if  the left can't do that if the enthusiasm  that was created when Donald Trump was  elected and inaugurated has sort of  faded and doesn't translate into people  actually getting out to vote if Trump  keeps successfully playing the victim  and it works and it keeps him raising  money then Trump gets reelected and as  I've done mathematically as I've  mathematically analyzed before Trump can  win reelection with only 18 to 19  percent of the voting public not of all  voters of people who actually vote but  of the voting age public Trump can win  reelection with only 18 or 19 percent of  their vote based on half the country not  voting  and based on the electoral college  system that we have so if Trump raising  a lot of money makes you realize we've  got to get our you-know-what together  for 2020 then buy it by all means that's  a good thing just understand some of  those fundraising numbers are pretty  overblown
 

